
= Naming Conventions and other methodological issues

This project involves excerpts from human rights courts and supervisory bodies. The official citation names for these documents is long and convoluted, and sometimes it eschews references to information that is crucial from the study such as the name of the issuing body or organ. To avoid excessively verbose subtitles, and to provide information for students, this casebook takes the approach of using a naming "handle" and combined with a bibliography. The name handle has the following format: (1) the issuing body, (2) a descriptive and easy to retrieve name of the document, (3) the date the document was issued. To compensate for the loss of precision, the bibliography section at the end will match this handle to the official citation of the document.

Another important issue is to clarify that this casebook does not try to reproduce the documents verbatim. This primarily involves extractive summarization, that will generally be denoted by skips in paragraph numbering or by ellipsis inside brackets ie. "[...]". Also the case book will not reproduce all the heading material and formatting of the official documents, or typographical choices such as all-caps or bold font. This is regarded as a strictly aesthetic issue were matching the official documents is not required. However the paragraph numbering, when present, is preserved in order to ensure the ability of the student to compare the excerpt with the original.

